{:edb/db-before-t 1724 :edb/db-after-t 1725 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535037 50 #inst "2026-10-08T15:27:11.616Z" 13194139535037 true] [17592186087520 1100 "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty$.\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1$?" 13194139535037 false] [17592186087520 1100 "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1?$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186087520 1145 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(-1)+3=1, \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}-4(-1)=-1+4=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3).$ The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/assets/0fa3fc12e83e2a581499680145df9229917c7426e6a3d12c51d88e115a42033e.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4 .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-4}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right).\n$$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186087520 1145 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}x & = 2 ( - 1 ) + 3 = 1 , \\\\ y & = ( - 1 {)}^{3} - 4 ( - 1 ) = - 1 + 4 = 3.\\end{array}$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3)$. The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/799/Source/Images/f8cfd5f61b0ad19f503ddb05927c280e.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTo compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$, the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}m = {\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} |}_{t = - 1} & = \\frac{3 ( - 1 {)}^{2} - 4}{2} = - \\frac{1}{2} .\\end{array}$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)\n$$\n\nand the slope\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right)\n$$" 13194139535037 false] [17592186087521 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/assets/9dcc4c97bd296ef4627b02b6cfafcdb643f475cd8dacd36a820db760bd6fb045.png)\n\nThe following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)." 13194139535037 true] [17592186087521 1100 "The following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n$x=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/799/Source/Images/b6d3cface8e385a6ebaa2fe18a25c564.png)\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)." 13194139535037 false] [17592186087521 1145 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ninto the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2\\cos (\\frac{\\pi }{4}) + \\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\sin (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} + 1, \\\\\ny &= \\cos (\\frac{\\pi }{4}) - 2\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2\\sin (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2(1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right)\n$$\n\nFor this, we need to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwhich means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} & = \\frac{{y}^{\\prime } ( t )}{{x}^{\\prime } ( t )} \\\\  & = \\frac{- \\sin   t - 4 \\cos   2 t}{- 2 \\sin   t + 2 \\cos   2 t} \\\\  & = - \\frac{\\sin   t + 4 \\cos   2 t}{2 ( \\cos   2 t - \\sin   t )} .\\end{array}$$\n\nAt the point where\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nthe slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}m & = {\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} |}_{t = \\pi  / 4} \\\\  & = - \\frac{\\sin   ( \\frac{\\pi }{4} ) + 4 \\cos   ( \\frac{\\pi }{2} )}{2 ( \\cos   ( \\frac{\\pi }{2} ) - \\sin   ( \\frac{\\pi }{4} ) )} \\\\  & = - \\frac{\\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{2} + 4 ( 0 )}{2 ( 0 - \\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{2} )} \\\\  & = \\frac{1}{2} .\\end{array}$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form\n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2) &= -2(x - (\\sqrt{2} + 1)) \\\\\ny - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 2 &= -2x + 2\\sqrt{2} + 2 \\\\\ny + 2x &= \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535037 false] [17592186087521 1145 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1, \\\\  \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right).$ For this, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}+4(0)}{2(0-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form \n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2,\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2) & =-2(x-(\\sqrt{2}+1)) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+2 & =-2x+2\\sqrt{2}+2 \\\\ y+2x & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186087529 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t^2+3, \\quad y=-t+1, \\qquad -\\infty < t < \\infty$.\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139535037 false] [17592186087529 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=t^2+3, \\quad y=-t+1, \\qquad -\\infty <     t <    \\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139535037 true] [17592186087529 1145 "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if $x'(t)=0$ while\n\n$$\ny'(t) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -1\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\ny'(0) = -1\\neq 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that when $t = 0$, the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139535037 false] [17592186087529 1145 "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0 .$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -1 .\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $y'(0) = -1\\neq 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t = 0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139535037 true] [17592186087530 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty$.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal." 13194139535037 false] [17592186087530 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal." 13194139535037 true] [17592186087530 1145 "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require $x'(t)=0$ while\n\n$$\ny'(t) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx^{\\prime }(t) = 6t^{2}, y^{\\prime }(t) = 2t - 1\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{\\prime }(0) &= 2 \\cdot 0 - 1 =-1 \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t = 0$." 13194139535037 false] [17592186087530 1145 "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2\\cdot 0-1=-1\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t = 0.$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186087699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328114 13194139535037 true] [17592186087699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328142 13194139535037 true] [17592186087699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328156 13194139535037 true] [17592186087699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328159 13194139535037 true] [17592186087700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328121 13194139535037 true] [17592186087700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328174 13194139535037 true] [17592186087700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328194 13194139535037 true] [17592186087700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328201 13194139535037 true] [17592186087709 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328149 13194139535037 true] [17592186087709 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328164 13194139535037 true] [17592186087709 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328169 13194139535037 true] [17592186087709 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328188 13194139535037 true] [17592186087710 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328128 13194139535037 true] [17592186087710 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328135 13194139535037 true] [17592186087710 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328179 13194139535037 true] [17592186087710 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328183 13194139535037 true] [17592186087960 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535037 true] [17592186087960 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\pi ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186087960 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = \\ln (\\sin t), y = \\frac{1}{\\sin t}, t \\in  (0, \\pi )$.\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t = \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C$." 13194139535037 false] [17592186087960 1145 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186088009 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535037 true] [17592186088009 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+7,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139535037 true] [17592186088009 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = 4\\sin t + 7, y = 4\\cos t + 10, 0 \\le t < 2\\pi $.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139535037 false] [17592186088009 1145 "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186088011 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535037 true] [17592186088011 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." 13194139535037 true] [17592186088011 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = e^{2t}, y = t^{2}, 0 \\le t \\le 2$.\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." 13194139535037 false] [17592186088011 1145 "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=2{e}^{0}=2\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186088214 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535037 true] [17592186088214 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/799/Source/Images/q-6857.png)\n\nA curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 2t^{3} - 3t, y = 3t^{2} - 5, - \\infty  < t < \\infty $.\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t = 1$." 13194139535037 false] [17592186088214 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}-5,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186088214 1145 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=2-3=-1, \\\\ y & =3(1)^{2}-5=3-5=-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6t}{6{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6\\cdot 1}{6\\cdot (1)^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6-3} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,-2)$ and the slope $m=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =2(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328114 1017 #edb/ref 17592186328115 13194139535037 true] [17592186328114 1089 "q-122698" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328114 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535037 true] [17592186328114 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{3t}+1,\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis." 13194139535037 true] [17592186328114 1113 #uuid "ff7ae082-6b60-5021-9c27-7d6fc5b0e34f" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328114 1145 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3\\cdot 0}+1=1+1=2, \\\\ y & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)=0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)),\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))}{3{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)}{3{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)}{3{e}^{2\\cdot 0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,0)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0-2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ is the $y$-intercept." 13194139535037 true] [17592186328115 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328116 13194139535037 true] [17592186328115 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328117 13194139535037 true] [17592186328115 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328118 13194139535037 true] [17592186328115 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328119 13194139535037 true] [17592186328115 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328120 13194139535037 true] [17592186328115 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535037 true] [17592186328115 1041 "selection" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328115 1104 #edb/ref 17592186328117 13194139535037 true] [17592186328115 1110 #uuid "cfe12377-8471-56c4-bd37-d7487894b69d" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328116 1034 "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328116 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535037 true] [17592186328116 1111 #uuid "dd97bb56-c6ba-56e1-a118-da23ec935a1e" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328117 1034 "(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328117 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535037 true] [17592186328117 1111 #uuid "fecad2d3-775c-5b11-a6db-515d5ea7f267" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328118 1034 "(0,-\\pi )" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328118 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535037 true] [17592186328118 1111 #uuid "42c4cde9-c8de-50ef-a9f9-58f74be1be94" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328119 1034 "(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328119 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535037 true] [17592186328119 1111 #uuid "647dc9d1-24cd-5c0c-9dd5-912079a1277a" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328120 1034 "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328120 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535037 true] [17592186328120 1111 #uuid "a50dbf58-3a9c-5229-ac9d-8301c2cb350c" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328121 1017 #edb/ref 17592186328122 13194139535037 true] [17592186328121 1089 "q-122898" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328121 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535037 true] [17592186328121 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-3t\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328121 1113 #uuid "430b8b34-04c8-52df-bdc6-a0f2f0e396d1" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328121 1145 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,-1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-3}{\\frac{2}{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{3}-3t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{3}-3(1)}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(\\frac{3}{2})\\,}=-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,-1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328122 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328123 13194139535037 true] [17592186328122 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328124 13194139535037 true] [17592186328122 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328125 13194139535037 true] [17592186328122 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328126 13194139535037 true] [17592186328122 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328127 13194139535037 true] [17592186328122 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535037 true] [17592186328122 1041 "selection" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328122 1104 #edb/ref 17592186328124 13194139535037 true] [17592186328122 1110 #uuid "350ef775-dd25-5e37-aaa5-22a1907149ff" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328123 1034 "y=\\frac{2}{3}x+1" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328123 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535037 true] [1759218