d the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328190 13194139535037 true] [17592186328189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328191 13194139535037 true] [17592186328189 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535037 true] [17592186328189 1041 "field-1" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328189 1104 #edb/ref 17592186328190 13194139535037 true] [17592186328189 1110 #uuid "8d85e419-b9be-540e-bf61-c5220a7f6ec3" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328190 1034 "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535037 true] [17592186328190 1111 #uuid "326352f5-3012-5dc9-a3d1-131cdf74ec4c" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328191 1034 "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328191 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535037 true] [17592186328191 1111 #uuid "a4c573bc-d647-5a9b-bd72-49595c7f390d" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328192 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328193 13194139535037 true] [17592186328192 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535037 true] [17592186328192 1041 "field-2" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328192 1104 #edb/ref 17592186328193 13194139535037 true] [17592186328192 1110 #uuid "536444a5-22cf-575c-b95c-99eb96d3bebe" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328193 1034 "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328193 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535037 true] [17592186328193 1111 #uuid "e8af08ec-710f-5106-800d-33b2a65b25b9" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328194 1017 #edb/ref 17592186328195 13194139535037 true] [17592186328194 1089 "q-6858" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328194 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535037 true] [17592186328194 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=2-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328194 1113 #uuid "8f364007-9bf0-5b90-a6c7-48aa10ff5533" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328194 1145 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={0}^{3}-3\\cdot 0=0-0=0, \\\\ y & =2-0=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {0}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{0-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(\\frac{1}{3})\\,}=-3\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-3(x-0) \\\\ y & =-3x+2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328195 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328196 13194139535037 true] [17592186328195 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328197 13194139535037 true] [17592186328195 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328198 13194139535037 true] [17592186328195 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328199 13194139535037 true] [17592186328195 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328200 13194139535037 true] [17592186328195 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535037 true] [17592186328195 1041 "selection" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328195 1104 #edb/ref 17592186328198 13194139535037 true] [17592186328195 1110 #uuid "84a9bb93-6316-547f-a2a0-5b05303dbe54" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328196 1034 "y=-\\,\\frac{12}{5}x+2" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328196 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535037 true] [17592186328196 1111 #uuid "d3b15a64-f05a-5f1f-87c8-cbcbdc4dc9d1" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328197 1034 "y=-\\,\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{8}{3}" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328197 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535037 true] [17592186328197 1111 #uuid "7f970631-df88-573f-9cac-544fd888abb3" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328198 1034 "y=-3x+2" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328198 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535037 true] [17592186328198 1111 #uuid "1d103378-e181-5a90-9206-e3134a0e8e26" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328199 1034 "y=-\\,\\frac{9}{4}x+2" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328199 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535037 true] [17592186328199 1111 #uuid "98f0488f-760b-5f04-b18d-7e054e2fd002" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328200 1034 "y=-3x+\\frac{12}{5}" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328200 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535037 true] [17592186328200 1111 #uuid "1ddbc08a-8d73-57f2-b1d2-8cd3d416345a" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328201 1017 #edb/ref 17592186328202 13194139535037 true] [17592186328201 1089 "q-75288" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328201 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535037 true] [17592186328201 1100 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,y=2{t}^{3}-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328201 1113 #uuid "f065fb78-5c08-5a67-9bfa-56a34450ed2a" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328201 1145 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1)^{4}-3(1)^{2}=-2, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}-6t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-1}{4{t}^{3}-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{2}-1}{4(1)^{3}-6(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\,-\\frac{5}{2}\\,)}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\frac{2}{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535037 true] [17592186328202 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328203 13194139535037 true] [17592186328202